---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const reqCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Elicit: ask the real users', note: 'Requirements come from people who rarely say what they mean. Watch the work, not just the wish list.' },
    { math: '2 · Separate functional from non-functional', note: '"The system sends invoices" is functional. "Invoices send in under a second, encrypted" is non-functional — and usually the harder promise.' },
    { math: '3 · Make them testable', note: '"Fast" is a wish; "p95 under 300 ms" is a requirement. If you cannot write the test, you have not written the requirement.' },
    { math: '4 · Specify, then validate', note: 'Write them down (stories, use cases, specs), then walk them back past the stakeholders — cheap now, ruinous later.' },
    { math: '5 · Expect change', note: 'Requirements are living artefacts. The engineering is not freezing them; it is managing their change without lying to anyone.' },
  ],
};

const costCfg = {
  points: [
    { points: [[1, 1], [2, 3], [3, 8], [4, 16], [5, 30], [6, 60]], color: '#b42318', label: 'relative cost to fix a requirements defect', mode: 'line' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.5, max: 6.5, label: 'phase found: 1 requirements → 6 production' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 70, label: 'relative cost (×)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/software-engineering/foundational/software-requirements">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Most software projects do not fail at coding — they fail at agreeing. Requirements engineering
    is the discipline of working out what to build, writing it down so it can be tested, and
    managing the fact that the answer will change. It is the least glamorous phase and the one that
    decides the project.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from elicitation to living specification" widget="equationStep" config={reqCfg} />

  <h2>The economics of finding defects early</h2>
  <p>
    A misunderstood requirement caught in a workshop costs a conversation. The same defect found in
    production costs releases, migrations and trust — industry studies since Boehm's work in the
    1970s–80s consistently show order-of-magnitude growth in repair cost per phase. That curve is
    the business case for the whole discipline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="defect cost grows phase by phase" widget="plotter" config={costCfg} />

  <h2>Functional versus non-functional</h2>
  <p>
    Functional requirements say what the system does: send invoices, reject duplicates. Non-
    functional requirements say how well: latency, throughput, security, accessibility. The second
    kind is where projects actually die, because it is invisible until it is broken — no demo ever
    fails on p95 latency.
  </p>

  <h2>User stories and the agile compromise</h2>
  <p>
    Agile methods replaced binding specifications with short statements — "as a customer, I want a
    refund button, so that…", with acceptance criteria attached. That trades completeness for
    adaptability, and works when the team can talk to users continuously. The engineering remains
    the same: make every claim testable, keep them current, never let silence stand for agreement.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SWEBOK Guide V4.0 (IEEE Computer Society), Requirements Engineering KA; Boehm, <em>Software Engineering Economics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
